  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 211 AM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nLingering showers/storms will be possible again over central Kansas \nand parts of southeast Kansas this morning. Current satellite water \nvapor imagery shows an embedded upper level short wave over Wyoming \ntraveling southeast via northwest flow regime aloft. This system \nwill track east-southeast across Nebraska today and reach southeast \nNebraska/northeast Kansas later tonight. A surface cold front will \ncontinue to migrate slowly southeast today and create a noticeable \ntemperature difference from central Kansas compared to southern \nKansas. A few storms could try and develop along the frontal \nboundary over southern Kansas late this afternoon/evening where \nconvective temperatures should be attained. Otherwise a better \nsignal for showers/storms will develop later tonight as low level \njet increases moisture transport into a 700mb baroclinic zone. \nExpecting scattered storms to begin developing over parts of \ncentral/south central Kansas and more numerous storms to impact \nnortheast Kansas/northwest Missouri mainly after 10pm. Elevated \nenvironment is supportive of a few strong to marginally severe \nstorms given favorable 700-500mb layer directional shear profile and \nadequate instability. The activity looks to shift east of Kansas by \nThursday morning with generally dry weather on tap during the day. \nAnother round of elevated showers/storms is expected Thursday night \nwith same signal of increasing low jet focusing moisture transport \ninto 700mb baroclinic zone. The coverage of showers/storms looks to \nbecome more numerous along with potential for a few elevated strong \nto marginally severe storms possible again. There is also a threat \nfor heavy rainfall to impact parts of southeast Kansas Thursday \nnight. \n\nDeterministic models continue to show upper level high pressure \ncenter stretching eastward from the Rockies across Kansas for the \nweekend through early next week. High confidence that another round \nof heat and dry weather will affect the region this period. Model \nensembles show increasing probabilities for 100 degrees or higher \ndaytime high temperatures to begin affecting locations generally \nalong and west of I-135/I-35 on Saturday. The model ensemble \nprobabilities for 100 degrees or greater for daytime highs reaches \n70-90% Sunday-Tuesday for those same locations.",
